WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.489 --> 00:00:04.460
Some Marvel Cinematic Universe stars have
put such a stamp on their characters that

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:06.960
it's become almost impossible to separate
the two.

00:00:07.609 --> 00:00:11.560
It's always kind of a shock to realize that
other actors might have almost been cast in

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:16.680
a role like Jason Momoa, who auditioned for
Drax before the role went to Dave Bautista

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:20.619
. We had a chance to sit down with Charlie
Wen - former head of visual development at

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:25.000
Marvel Studios- for an exclusive interview
to discuss the evolution of Drax's cinematic

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:30.500
design, how Jason Momoa almost joined the
MCU...and how the character nearly ended up

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:33.200
looking like a very famous video game character.

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:39.120
When describing the original 1973 comic book
design for Drax the Destroyer, you might use

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:42.660
terms like "simple," "distinct," and "extremely
recognizable."

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:48.400
You might also add the terms "unbearably ridiculous"
and "monumentally goofy" to that list.

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:50.480
The '70s were a different time, man.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:52.000
"Cocaine's a hell of a drug."

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:59.320
Overall, Drax's outfit when he first appeared
in Iron Man #55 is unforgettable...and also

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:03.700
impossible to put on the big screen without
causing the audience to barf into their popcorn.

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:08.620
Naturally, Wen and his team at Marvel Studios
never got anywhere close to these looks when

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:10.720
they began adapting Drax for the screen.

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:16.020
Before becoming the head of visual development
at Marvel Studios, Charlie Wen also designed

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:20.160
Kratos, the murderous hero from the God of
War video game franchise, for Sony.

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:24.480
The game first debuted in March 2005 after
a few years of development.

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:29.700
Meanwhile, later that year, Marvel Comics
happened to release a Drax the Destroyer mini-series

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:31.460
with a brand new design for the character.

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:34.920
Gone were the goofy purple tights and skull
accessories.

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:38.260
Instead, Drax ditched the shirt and got some
ink.

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:43.020
While the design was new to Drax, it definitely
had more than a few similarities with Wen's

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:47.700
final Kratos design — something he'd discovered
when someone sent the company a Drax comic

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:48.200
book.

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:56.719
"The funny part with Drax is that he ... When
I was working on God of War back in the day,

00:01:56.719 --> 00:02:02.170
somebody had sent me, or sent Sony, a Drax
comic book.

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:02.660
And…

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:05.140
The thing is, we're like, 'Oh wow.

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:07.360
That looks a lot like Kratos.'

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:08.920
I remember that.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:13.840
I remember at Sony, we were like, 'Oh man,
what should we do about this?

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:17.280
They can't make it look' ... It looked so
much like Kratos!"

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:22.020
So it came as a surprise when, just a few
years later, Wen led the design department

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:26.170
at Marvel Studios...and found himself face-to-face
with the challenge of having to adapt the

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:29.640
cosmic hero who already resembled a character
he'd designed before.

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:31.740
"Then fast forward.

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:35.220
I'm like, 'Oh, this is that character.'

00:02:35.221 --> 00:02:36.221
I didn't realize that.

00:02:36.221 --> 00:02:40.800
Then it was like, how am I gonna make this
character not look like the character I designed

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:41.800
for God of War?"

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:46.880
As Wen led his team through designs for Guardians
of the Galaxy, the film's casting directors

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:48.200
hunted for the right actors.

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:53.280
One up-and-comer named Jason Momoa who'd found
success with his portrayal of Khal Drogo in

00:02:53.290 --> 00:02:57.070
HBO's Game of Thrones became a frontrunner
to win the part of Drax.

00:02:57.070 --> 00:03:05.970
"So, Jason Momoa sent in this amazing screen
test, and of course he already knew how to

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:07.400
do those fighting positions.

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:08.980
He looked very convincing."

00:03:09.310 --> 00:03:13.760
Wen created art depicting the actor in the
role, featuring the Momoa's face on a lean-looking

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:18.500
Drax the Destroyer that's a clear step away
from the Kratos-looking comics and much closer

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:20.080
to the final cinematic design.

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:25.120
As it turned out, however, while Momoa had
the look and the moves to play Drax, he wasn't

00:03:25.130 --> 00:03:29.390
quite right for the part at least not when
compared with another actor vying for the

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:31.300
role, wrestler Dave Bautista.

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:34.840
"In a way it's like it didn't push that character
the way that Bautista did.”

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:36.680
"That was the worst pep talk.

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:37.660
Ever."

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:42.940
"I think with Momoa in it, it felt like the
Game of Thrones character that he played.

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:46.010
He has a great face for it.

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:54.080
We did some takes with Momoa as the character,
as Drax, but ultimately I think Bautista really

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:55.160
took that role.

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:58.480
From that point on, too, it's like we designed
it specifically for him."

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:03.320
Momoa himself seemed to agree that his casting
wouldn't bring much that was new to the screen,

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:07.220
revealing why he ultimately turned down the
role in a 2014 interview:

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:09.460
"I want my children to see their father happy.

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:12.600
It's not that it's not a good role, it just
wasn't the right thing.

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:17.180
I was on Stargate: Atlantis for four years
playing a similar character called Ronon,

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:19.760
who was an alien who didn't say much and grunted.

00:04:19.769 --> 00:04:22.409
I've been there and done that, whether people
have seen it or not.

00:04:22.409 --> 00:04:23.280
You want to stretch."

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:28.039
Of course, no one could know back in 2014
that Momoa would go on to headline his very

00:04:28.039 --> 00:04:33.620
own superhero movie a few years later with
2018's Aquaman, one of the tentpole films

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:36.500
of the DC Extended Universe — but that's
another story.

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:41.720
So Momoa was out, and Bautista was in, and
both Wen and Marvel movie fans would agree

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:43.659
that the right man was cast in the part.

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:49.780
“You know, Bautista brought this, sort of,
a different side to it that nobody was expecting,

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:52.860
a little funny, but naturally funny.

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:55.580
It was like, wow this is a whole character.

00:04:55.580 --> 00:04:56.960
He's bringing something to that character.

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:04.620
"I've mastered the ability of standing so
incredibly still that I've become invisible

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:05.460
to the eye.

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:07.300
Watch."

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:10.860
"Ow!

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:11.780
My nipples!

00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:13.220
Wauugghhhhh!"

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:16.800
"From that point on, it's like we designed
it specifically for him.

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:18.560
Even the way his head is shaped."

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:22.840
If you take a look at one of the first pieces
of concept art Kevin Feige unveiled to announce

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:28.800
Guardians of the Galaxy, you can definitely
see the influence of Bautista himself on the

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:29.800
character's design.

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:35.020
Meanwhile, to further evolve Drax's cinematic
look, Wen continued pushing the design away

00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:39.780
from the clean lines of the mid-2000s Drax
the one that had looked so much like Kratos.

00:05:40.010 --> 00:05:44.640
Instead, he focused on making Drax's tattoos
an integral part of the adapted character's

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:45.680
internal life.

00:05:45.980 --> 00:05:49.660
“All the tattoos that went on him is really
telling about his story.

00:05:49.669 --> 00:05:51.590
His life is basically on his body.

00:05:51.590 --> 00:05:53.949
And so, it's very complex.

00:05:53.949 --> 00:05:59.310
It's not something that you would ever do
for maybe a comic book or a video game because

00:05:59.310 --> 00:06:02.800
it's just harder to read as an instant read.

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:09.740
But we definitely put more into his story
with just his look, as well as pulled it away

00:06:09.740 --> 00:06:11.280
from Kratos."

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:15.720
It's easy to see in this image that also features
early versions of the other Guardians that

00:06:15.729 --> 00:06:19.830
the tattoos are beginning to feature even
more prominently in Drax's design — and

00:06:19.830 --> 00:06:21.080
his intimidation factor.

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:24.900
"Character trait wise, he's not the most intelligent
character in the world,

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:26.700
"Ha ha ha!

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:28.980
I have famously huge turds!"

00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:36.200
but he's ... I think he's surprisingly loyal,
but he's got his own sense of honor, you know

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:39.880
brutal honor that he lives by, his own brutal
code.

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:43.319
Drax is just, everybody's afraid of him.

00:06:43.319 --> 00:06:46.669
He's somebody that even people in prison are
afraid of.

00:06:46.669 --> 00:06:51.650
This is like an intergalactic prison, where
you have all these huge characters that would,

00:06:51.650 --> 00:06:54.289
I don't know, just look really scary and monstrous.

00:06:54.289 --> 00:06:58.500
But Drax is somebody that can definitely hold
his own to any of them."

